: Tell me about a time a pipeline failed in production.

What happened: We get a file every day from the order system. Each row has a customer ID, a KLS_ID (which tells us the address or pincode), and product info (like ADSL, VDSL, or a plan such as VDSL-100MBPS). One morning, the report that shows "which orders are ready for installation" didn't update. Field engineers use this report to plan their daily visits, so this was a problem — they had no idea which new orders needed a visit that day.

How I found the cause: I went into Databricks and checked which step failed. I found that the step which processes and cleans the data had crashed. When I looked closer, I saw that a few orders had come in with the product info missing — this happens when a customer places an order but hasn't finalized their exact plan yet. Our code was written assuming product info would always have a value, so when it saw a blank value, it broke the entire batch, not just those few orders.

What I did to fix it: I first fixed the immediate problem — I changed the code so that if product info is missing, that order goes into a separate "pending" table instead of stopping everything. Then I manually reran the missed day's data so field engineers got their list back with almost no delay.

What I did after, to prevent it again: I didn't want the same mistake to repeat, so I added an automatic check that looks for missing or incomplete product info before the main processing even starts. If it finds a problem, it sends an alert to the team right away, instead of letting the whole pipeline crash. Since then, this exact issue hasn't happened again, and the check has also caught a couple of similar problems early.
