Describe a challenging bug you debugged.

What happened: The team noticed something strange in our report — the same customer, at the same address, was showing up twice, with two different statuses. One row said "in progress" and another said "completed." It looked like a data mistake, and the business team was confused about whether the customer actually had one order or two.

How I investigated: I first checked if it was a simple duplicate — like the same file being loaded twice by mistake. That wasn't the case. So I looked closer at the actual data for that customer and realized what happened: the customer had asked to upgrade their plan — for example, from VDSL-50MBPS to VDSL-100MBPS — before their original installation was even completed. This created a new order record, with the same customer ID and same address, but a different product info value.

What the real issue was: Our code was designed to remove duplicate records by looking only at customer ID and address, and keeping just the latest one. Since it didn't check the product info, it treated the plan upgrade as if it were a duplicate of the original order, and deleted the important change instead of keeping both.

How I fixed it: I updated the logic so it also considers the product/plan info when deciding what counts as a duplicate. This way, if the plan changes, it's treated as a real update, not a duplicate to be thrown away. As a side benefit, this also gave the business a clear history of plan upgrades, which they didn't have before and later asked us to build further.
